# recruited — the outcome, not the verb.
#
# cli/verb/tame/ proves the chip is printed. This proves the chip corresponds to something
# real: the world is inspected before and after, and it moved. The verb script runs in full
# here — the two overlap on purpose, same scenario, different question.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --location-type test --location-id 0 --npc-static --start-area "Test Den" --period noon --observe-only wolf

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60

# --- before ---
inspect party
advance 12 180

# The beast is there and hostile: the phase opens on it under the threat rule.
expect wolf
state

# --- appease: enemy -> suspicious, which is what tame requires --------------------
strategy succeed
goal appease
click keyword wolf
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect Appeasement

# --- close the segment; the beast is still in the scene and still observable ------
click button
wait 60
advance 12 180
expect wolf

# --- tame: the beast joins the party ---------------------------------------------
# Difficulty 4 off a three-modus-mentis chain: `strategy succeed` asks for more sixes
# than there are dice, which used to hang the game outright (NarrativeController caps it).
goal tame
click keyword wolf
choose 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click action 0
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect Joined you

# --- after: gone from the scene, present in the party ----------------------------
# The next segment re-applies the period (StartObservationPhaseWithHistory → ApplyTimePeriod),
# which re-places the scene's NPCs — the tamed beast is no longer among them, so it cannot be
# observed or acted on again.
#
# That half is NOT asserted on screen: `expect` scans the whole terminal, and the beast's name
# is all over the greyed history of the two segments that just tamed it. The signal is on stdout
# instead — with --observe-only still pinned to the beast, this phase prints
#   [debug] --observe-only 'wolf': nothing here matches — offering the whole scene.
# and `state` drops one observable (observed=1/10 before the tame, 3/9 after).
goal none
click button
wait 60
advance 12 180
state

# The party side: the management screen lists the beast under P A R T Y, and that is all this
# asserts. The roster prints a NAME and no species, and a beast's name comes from the name
# generator — content, free to change, and a test that spells one breaks the day somebody adds a
# syllable. That the beast is really in the party is proven above by "Joined you", and that it is
# really out of the scene by the observable count dropping across the tame.
manage
wait 20
expect P A R T Y
manage
wait 20

# --- and it stays gone: leave the location, come back, the scene is rebuilt ------
# The rebuild is a pure function of the location id, so without the departed record in
# LocationInstanceState the very same wolf would be standing here again. stdout shows
#   SceneFactory: '<the beast>' left this location for good — not rebuilt
click button
wait mode WorldView 120
advance 12 120
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 120
advance 12 180
state

# After a successful tame the beast is recruited and gone from the location for good, so a later
# phase's `--observe-only wolf` matching nothing is this script's whole point — the observable count
# dropping across the tame is the assertion. Declared expected so the strict flag check does not
# read the success as a misfire.
allow-flag-miss --observe-only
# The outcome TYPE, asserted by its stable id rather than by chip wording. `expect <chip>`
# proves what the player was told; this proves what actually ran. See --outcome-audit.
expect-outcome recruited


# --- the outcome really ran ---
expect-outcome recruited

# --- after: the world moved ---
inspect party
expect-state party companion
quit
